WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Sanitizer builds should fit cleanly into the normal C++ build strategy.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:12.040
A common practice is to create dedicated configurations for sanitizer work rather than modifying production release builds directly.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:19.660
That separation helps teams keep release settings stable while making instrumented builds easy for developers and CI systems to run.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:27.940
At a high level, sanitizer configurations add compiler and linker options that enable instrumentation and link the sanitizer runtime.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.480
They should also include useful debug symbols so reports point back to source locations.

6
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.900
Some teams adjust optimization levels or preserve frame pointers where that makes stack traces more reliable.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.240
The exact build details vary by compiler, platform, and project, but the goal is the same: make the sanitizer build repeatable, visible, and easy to compare across machines.

8
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.800
Sanitizers add runtime and memory overhead.

9
00:00:53.900 --> 00:01:01.820
They can also expose compatibility issues with third-party dependencies, custom allocators, unusual build modes, or platform-specific code.

10
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.200
That overhead is normal.

11
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:12.180
These builds are typically used in development, pull-request validation, scheduled CI, testing, and staging workflows.

12
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:20.360
Production deployment with sanitizers is a specialized decision that requires controlled operational reasoning, not a default assumption.

13
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.400
Good build hygiene matters.

14
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:29.780
Document which sanitizers are enabled, which targets participate, how dependencies are built, and how reports should be collected.

15
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:37.720
If developers can reproduce the same sanitizer configuration locally, they can fix findings faster and trust CI failures more.

16
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.080
It is also wise to keep sanitizer configuration close to the build system rather than relying on private command lines.

17
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:50.740
Named presets, documented targets, or repeatable CI jobs reduce confusion about which flags were used.

18
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:58.600
When a report comes from a known configuration, engineers spend less time debating the environment and more time investigating the defect.
